WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:05.171
In this
lesson you will train several tokenizers.

2
00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:09.976
Since they determine how the transformer
model processes input text,

3
00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.646
it is important to give them
extra attention.

4
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.950
All right. Let's build something.

5
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.052
Tokenizers are trainable components

6
00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:22.455
and they learn the best vocabulary
for given training data.

7
00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:26.459
There isn't a single definition
of what "the best" means in that case.

8
00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:29.662
And various tokenization algorithms exist.

9
00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:33.466
Contrary to neural networks,
the tokenizer training process

10
00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:37.570
is fully deterministic, and it's based on
the statistics of the input data.

11
00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:41.207
Various models
choose different tokenization methods.

12
00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:45.111
OpenAI prefers byte per encoding,

13
00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:49.082
while WordPiece is quite
commonly used by some other providers,

14
00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:52.485
including open-source sentence
transformers, that you use.

15
00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:56.389
However, Cohere select work
best for their English model,

16
00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.826
but Unigram to create a multilingual one.

17
00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:03.430
The size of the vocabulary
is a hyperparameter we need to choose

18
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:08.430
upfront, and it's usually at least
30,000 tokens. For the multilingual models,

19
00:01:08.635 --> 00:01:12.272
that might be even a few times more,
but that's expectable

20
00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:15.241
as the set of characters
to support is way wider,

21
00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:18.344
and there are also more sequences
to cover.

22
00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:19.012
Let's check

23
00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:23.116
the most popular tokenization algorithms
and see how they split the text.

24
00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:26.619
Byte per encoding is a common choice.

25
00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:31.357
BPE starts by splitting the input
by whitespace characters.

26
00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:35.095
Natural boundaries
defined by words are kept.

27
00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:40.228
So a single token
will never overlap two words. The vocabulary

28
00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:43.970
is initialized with all the characters
in the training set.

29
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.308
New tokens are iteratively created
by merging

30
00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:52.479
two tokens, which are the most often
put next to each other.

31
00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:56.783
The most common part is selected
and added into the vocabulary,

32
00:01:57.016 --> 00:02:00.120
but we do not remove
the tokens used to create it.

33
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.157
There are kept in the vocabulary,
so we can also use them to tokenize

34
00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.725
some other words.

35
00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:09.496
Initially,
our sentence is split into single letters,

36
00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:12.932
but after the first step, two consecutive

37
00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:15.935
tokens are merged to form another one.

38
00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:20.440
The process continues until we reach
the desired vocabulary size.

39
00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:25.078
We select the most common pair of tokens
from the previous step,

40
00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:28.581
and then add the new token
into the vocabulary.

41
00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:33.453
The number of steps depends
on the number of tokens we want to have.

42
00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:36.623
If we set the vocabulary size to 14,

43
00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:40.026
this is how the final vocabulary
will look like.

44
00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:44.130
Let's repeat that with one of the existing
tokenizer implementations

45
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.565
from HuggingFace.

46
00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:48.234
The HuggingFace Tokenizers
library provides

47
00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:51.237
implementations of various tokenization
algorithms.

48
00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:54.207
You will train multiple ones

49
00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:57.710
using the same
very simple training data set.

50
00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:00.613
Implementing byte per

51
00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:03.616
encoding
is as easy as running a few imports

52
00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:06.753
and gluing the components together.

53
00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:15.395
A tokenizer is a general component
that requires

54
00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:18.932
a selected tokenization model
to be passed as an argument.

55
00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:24.237
It also allows setting some tokenization
that will be run on each input text

56
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.240
before we really start tokenizing it.

57
00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:29.709
Whitespace pre-tokenization helps

58
00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:32.712
to split the text into words.

59
00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:34.681
The last thing you will set

60
00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:38.651
is the trainer object
and the size of a target vocabulary.

61
00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:42.255
Practically, you will never go below
at least a few thousand,

62
00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:45.425
but in this case, 14 should be just fine.

63
00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:47.827
You can also experiment
with different values.

64
00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:51.397
You will now train the tokenizer
from a Python iterator

65
00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:55.802
by passing the training set
with a corresponding trainer instance.

66
00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:02.041
Then you can get the vocabulary

67
00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:05.044
to see what are the tokens learned.

68
00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:09.949
The training process is iterative,
so we can see what was the order

69
00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:13.853
of adding new tokens into the vocabulary
simply by checking their IDs.

70
00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:17.857
This is how our training example

71
00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:20.860
would be tokenized.

72
00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.297
Created tokenizer

73
00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:27.300
might be also used to encode
some new text.

74
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.970
Let's say we had a typo and we want to see

75
00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:35.141
the output tokens for this text.

76
00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:39.812
Obviously, IDs are not that easy to read,

77
00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:43.082
so let's access tokens instead.

78
00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:47.287
Another interesting thing is to
what will happen

79
00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:52.191
if we pass some letters
that didn't occur in the training phase.

80
00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:56.896
The letters S and H
has never been seen during this process,

81
00:04:57.163 --> 00:05:01.868
so they are omitted in the output
as no corresponding token is available.

82
00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:04.971
Byte pair encoding is
commonly used in large language models,

83
00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:08.207
but typically on a byte,
not character level.

84
00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:10.843
Wordpiece is a similar algorithm

85
00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:13.846
and it's quite
often used in an embedded models.

86
00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:16.649
The main difference between BPE and WordPiece

87
00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:20.486
is that the latter distinguishes
between the first letters of words

88
00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:25.753
and the middle letters by appending,
double hash prefix to each middle letter.

89
00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:29.862
The general idea of WordPiece
is to learn words, prefixes,

90
00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:34.000
and suffixes separately, as prefixes
are thought to keep the meaning

91
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.470
which the inflected suffix does not change
much.

92
00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:39.038
Words:

93
00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:44.038
Walk, walking, walked, and walks
all refer to the same activity,

94
00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:47.547
so we expect the model to treat them
similarly.

95
00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:50.717
Ideally,
one token should represent an abstract

96
00:05:50.717 --> 00:05:54.587
concept of walking
and the next should represent the tense.

97
00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.090
This is how the initial

98
00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.093
vocabulary of the word
tokenizer would look like.

99
00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:04.430
The training process starts
with a vocabulary built from each word's

100
00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:08.801
letters and the middle letters of words
prefixed with double hash.

101
00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:14.469
Then the algorithm iteratively merges
the pairs of tokens, but it selects

102
00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:19.674
the tokens to merge based on the score,
which is different compared to BPE.

103
00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:24.083
We do not select the most frequent part
anymore, but also consider

104
00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:27.787
how often these two tokens occur
in other contexts.

105
00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:30.790
If we calculate the scores
for each pair of tokens

106
00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:35.790
in the first iteration, it becomes evident
that if two tokens always ocurre

107
00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:40.466
next to each other,
they will be selected for merge.

108
00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:43.936
The word "long" consists of letters

109
00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:46.939
we cannot find anywhere else
in the training data.

110
00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:49.876
Similarly to BPE, we add new

111
00:06:49.876 --> 00:06:52.879
tokens iteratively. In the same example,

112
00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:56.082
our tokenization already works differently

113
00:06:56.082 --> 00:06:59.085
and prefers different pairs of tokens.

114
00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:04.056
Again, the process will stop
when we reach a desired vocabulary size.

115
00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:07.260
Here are the steps performed

116
00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:10.263
when we set the vocabulary size to 27.

117
00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:15.268
Since WordPiece adds all the single
letters and middle letters as separate

118
00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:20.173
tokens, we usually need a bigger
vocabulary to capture these regularities.

119
00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.677
Let's implement the tokenizer again
for the same training set.

120
00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:26.712
The HuggingFace Tokenizers

121
00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:29.715
library has a slightly different
implementation of WordPiece.

122
00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:33.786
Instead of calculating the scores,
it always selects the most common

123
00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:34.854
pair of tokens,

124
00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:38.191
similarly to BPE. But differentiates

125
00:07:38.191 --> 00:07:41.828
the middle letters by attaching the double
hash prefix to them.

126
00:07:41.928 --> 00:07:45.331
Due to that,
you will now use another library

127
00:07:45.565 --> 00:07:48.334
implemented specifically for this course.

128
00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:52.472
It is based on the HuggingFace
Tokenizers and provides a real-world

129
00:07:52.705 --> 00:07:56.375
implementation
using score to choose the tokens to merge.

130
00:07:56.776 --> 00:08:01.647
This time the target
size of the vocabulary is set to 27.

131
00:08:02.248 --> 00:08:02.915
The real world WordPiece

132
00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:07.453
library is not 100% compatible
with HuggingFace Tokenizers

133
00:08:07.487 --> 00:08:10.490
in terms
of how we run the training process.

134
00:08:10.823 --> 00:08:15.328
This time you have to call the trainers
train tokenizer method

135
00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:17.997
and pass the

136
00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:21.100
training
data, as well as the tokenizer instance

137
00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.103
you want to train.

138
00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.104
Our vocabulary

139
00:08:25.104 --> 00:08:28.107
already looks different
than the case of BPE.

140
00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:33.813
Let's check how our training example
will be now converted into tokens.

141
00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:38.351
The training procedure
was able to find a prefix that occurs

142
00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:42.355
multiple times,
and it sounds like a desired behavior.

143
00:08:42.488 --> 00:08:46.626
When it comes to typos,
you shouldn't notice any huge changes.

144
00:08:47.026 --> 00:08:50.830
If you now pass the text
with some unknown characters,

145
00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:55.368
then the results will be a bit different.

146
00:08:57.203 --> 00:08:58.237
Our tokenizer

147
00:08:58.237 --> 00:09:01.240
throws an error
when it finds an unknown character.

148
00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:05.645
Thus, we need to specify
a special fallback unknown token.

149
00:09:06.078 --> 00:09:09.115
It has to be passed on to the model
and the trainer.

150
00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:10.283
You will now back

151
00:09:10.283 --> 00:09:12.451
use the WordPiece training as implemented

152
00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:15.688
in HuggingFace library,
but with this unknown token used.

153
00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:19.759
You can also experiment
and use the real-world WordPiece train procedure.

154
00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:22.261
See how the results will differ.

155
00:09:22.261 --> 00:09:25.264
You will now define
the same training procedure

156
00:09:25.798 --> 00:09:28.367
using HuggingFace Tokenizers,

157
00:09:28.367 --> 00:09:31.270
but with the unknown token used.
It has to be

158
00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:34.273
known by both the model and the trainer.

159
00:09:34.273 --> 00:09:37.944
We'll also increase
the target size of the vocabulary by one,

160
00:09:38.411 --> 00:09:41.914
as the unknown token will also get its own
ID as well.

161
00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:44.517
Let's run the training process again.

162
00:09:47.787 --> 00:09:48.421
The learn

163
00:09:48.421 --> 00:09:51.390
vocabulary is different than before,

164
00:09:51.424 --> 00:09:53.759
and so will be the tokenization

165
00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:56.762
for the same examples.

166
00:09:57.797 --> 00:09:59.398
If we now run

167
00:09:59.398 --> 00:10:03.002
the newly created tokenizer
on the same training example,

168
00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:07.206
the output should be already a bit
different.

169
00:10:07.206 --> 00:10:10.876
The HuggingFace
variant seems to cover full words

170
00:10:11.577 --> 00:10:14.580
with individual tokens a bit better.

171
00:10:19.585 --> 00:10:21.988
For typos,

172
00:10:21.988 --> 00:10:25.291
we still shouldn't see any major change.

173
00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:28.728
However, when we pass some unknown tokens,

174
00:10:30.296 --> 00:10:31.998
they will be automatically

175
00:10:31.998 --> 00:10:35.001
converted into the special token
we created.

176
00:10:35.368 --> 00:10:37.970
We could obviously select
a different value for it,

177
00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.973
but that's a typical convention.

178
00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:41.574
BPE and WordPiece

179
00:10:41.574 --> 00:10:44.777
are not the only algorithms,
but they have much in common,

180
00:10:45.478 --> 00:10:48.848
especially because they start
with a basic set of characters

181
00:10:48.848 --> 00:10:52.985
and then iteratively merge them to form
more complex tokens.

182
00:10:53.753 --> 00:10:56.288
Unigram has a different approach.

183
00:10:56.288 --> 00:10:59.158
Instead of building the tokens
from the bottom up,

184
00:10:59.158 --> 00:11:01.827
we can also do it the other way around.

185
00:11:01.827 --> 00:11:04.864
Unigram starts with a huge vocabulary,
quite often

186
00:11:04.864 --> 00:11:09.301
created with BPE, using a way
bigger vocabulary size than expected,

187
00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:13.773
and then it removes some of the tokens
based on the calculated loss.

188
00:11:14.073 --> 00:11:18.611
The initial vocabulary allows to tokenize
a single word in multiple ways.

189
00:11:19.979 --> 00:11:22.982
The set of all the possible tokenization

190
00:11:22.982 --> 00:11:26.619
is important to calculate the loss
in Unigram training process.

191
00:11:27.219 --> 00:11:32.219
At each iteration, the algorithm computes
how much the overall loss would increase

192
00:11:32.892 --> 00:11:36.462
if a specific token was removed
and looks for the tokens

193
00:11:36.462 --> 00:11:38.431
that would increase it the least.

194
00:11:38.431 --> 00:11:41.701
Probabilities are defined
by the frequencies of the tokens.

195
00:11:42.201 --> 00:11:47.139
In our case, there are 63 occurrences
of the tokens in total.

196
00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:50.810
That means we can calculate
the probability of a particular

197
00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:53.813
tokenization as a product of frequencies

198
00:11:53.813 --> 00:11:56.949
of all the tokens
used. Even in such a simple example,

199
00:11:56.982 --> 00:12:00.252
the number of tokenization to consider
is pretty significant.

200
00:12:00.453 --> 00:12:04.190
Unigram training will check
how removing each individual

201
00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:08.194
token will impact the loss,
and then select the one that

202
00:12:08.194 --> 00:12:11.864
might be removed with the smallest impact
on the loss value.

203
00:12:12.098 --> 00:12:15.901
Analyzing each step of the unigram
training process would require lots

204
00:12:15.901 --> 00:12:16.969
of computations.

205
00:12:16.969 --> 00:12:20.773
Practically, we can train
the Unigram model with the HuggingFace

206
00:12:20.773 --> 00:12:23.776
tokenizers library
without changing too much.

207
00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:27.313
The Unigram model is the last
that we will train today.

208
00:12:29.014 --> 00:12:29.515
The training

209
00:12:29.515 --> 00:12:32.952
pipeline is similar to any other model
you tried so far.

210
00:12:33.652 --> 00:12:37.490
We expect the vocabulary size of 14 tokens.

211
00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:40.392
You might be surprised
that the vocabulary is shorter

212
00:12:40.392 --> 00:12:44.864
than the expected size of 14,
but that's just an implementation detail.

213
00:12:45.097 --> 00:12:48.901
Unigram removes a couple of tokens
at each step and only guarantees

214
00:12:48.901 --> 00:12:52.505
that the size of the vocabulary
won't be bigger than desired.

215
00:12:53.072 --> 00:12:56.342
We can see the common prefixes
were found to be important,

216
00:12:56.575 --> 00:13:00.813
so the hope is that our tokenization
can capture the meaning correctly.

217
00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:04.683
Also,
there are no double-letter tokens anymore.

218
00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.753
What was the case with WordPiece and BPE?

219
00:13:07.753 --> 00:13:12.191
We only have the base alphabet
and the common prefixes of the words.

220
00:13:12.625 --> 00:13:15.995
Unigram seems to be a solution
to the problem of so-called glitch

221
00:13:15.995 --> 00:13:17.663
tokens, as it removes the ones

222
00:13:17.663 --> 00:13:20.666
which won't be using the tokenization
of the training data,

223
00:13:20.733 --> 00:13:23.903
while the other methods
keep them in the vocabulary.

224
00:13:24.737 --> 00:13:28.541
We can also try out the tokenization
on the same examples

225
00:13:28.541 --> 00:13:31.544
as we used
before to see the output tokens.

226
00:13:32.311 --> 00:13:34.580
Similarly, if we make a typo

227
00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:38.250
or when we pass data
with some unknown tokens.

228
00:13:39.685 --> 00:13:42.087
In this case,
the unknown letters seem to be supported

229
00:13:42.087 --> 00:13:45.724
when we check the tokens,
so let's see their identifiers.

230
00:13:46.091 --> 00:13:50.162
A know token is used for the sequences
of unknown characters,

231
00:13:50.162 --> 00:13:53.165
but checking the tokens does not show it
clearly.

232
00:13:53.499 --> 00:13:56.135
If you read some other materials
about tokenization,

233
00:13:56.135 --> 00:13:59.605
you can easily find sentence
peace to be mentioned quite often.

234
00:14:00.072 --> 00:14:03.475
Let's see how it differs
from the other methods we discussed today.

235
00:14:04.243 --> 00:14:08.581
SentencePiece is just an implementation
of the same tokenization algorithms, with

236
00:14:08.581 --> 00:14:12.852
one additional assumption about the text,
not a different algorithm itself.

237
00:14:13.252 --> 00:14:16.522
SentencePiece
does not text by white spaces,

238
00:14:16.522 --> 00:14:19.525
but treats them like any other characters.

239
00:14:19.959 --> 00:14:22.328
That enables it to work for languages

240
00:14:22.328 --> 00:14:25.497
that do not use them as separators.
Internally,

241
00:14:26.131 --> 00:14:30.402
SentencePiece uses byte per encoding
or Unigram to build the vocabulary.

242
00:14:30.669 --> 00:14:34.940
SentencePiece allows building tokens
that span across multiple words.

243
00:14:35.241 --> 00:14:38.110
The fact that it allows
whitespace characters to be parts of

244
00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:39.845
the tokens is essential.

245
00:14:39.845 --> 00:14:43.582
For example, if you want to build
an embedding model for code. Languages

246
00:14:43.582 --> 00:14:46.719
like Python,
for which in dictation matters a lot

247
00:14:46.719 --> 00:14:49.688
in terms of meaning
will benefit from this approach.

248
00:14:50.289 --> 00:14:54.093
However, there are also English data
in which some proper names

249
00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:58.297
may contain multiple words
like San Francisco or Real Madrid.

250
00:14:59.265 --> 00:15:01.467
Having a single token for

251
00:15:01.467 --> 00:15:04.937
both words
might be beneficial in some cases.

252
00:15:05.571 --> 00:15:08.841
Checking the tokenization
algorithm and its vocabulary

253
00:15:08.841 --> 00:15:11.844
is an important step
in choosing the model.

254
00:15:12.111 --> 00:15:14.647
The practical implications
might be still unclear,

255
00:15:14.647 --> 00:15:17.950
but we will try to shed some light
on them in the next lesson.

